    "markdown": "## Word order in present simple questions\n\nThe [order of words](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/) in an English question in present simple is **QWASI**: (**Question Word) + Auxiliary + Subject + Infinitive**.\n\n-   *What do you do?*\n-   *Where do they live?*\n\nIn **Yes-No questions** (questions that we answer saying **yes** or **no**) there is **NO Question Word**.\n\n-   *Do you speak English?*\n-   *Does she live here?*\n\nThe **auxiliary verb** in present simple is **be** or **do/does**. Below, you can see a grammar chart on the order of words in present simple questions with **be** or **do/does**.  \n  \n![Grammar chart for A1 elementary English learners showing the word order of Present Simple questions with 'be' and 'do', including question words, auxiliary verbs, subjects, and main verbs.](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-1.png)  \n\n#### Questions with **am/is/are**\n\nWhen the verb in the sentence is **[be](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/)**, we use **am/is/are** as the auxiliary verb **before** the subject. Then the word order is **QWAS**: (**Question Word) + Auxiliary (am/is/are) + Subject**\n\n#### Questions with **do/does**\n\nWhen we have a verb that is not **be**, we use **do/does** as the auxiliary verb.  \nWe use **does** with **he/she/it** and **do** with **I/you/we/they**. Remember that the main verb after the subject is in the infinitive form (NOT **\\-s** or **\\-es**).\n\n-   *What do**es** she **do**? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-2.png)* \n-   *What does she **does**? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-3.png)* \n-   *Where do**es** he **work**? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-4.png)* \n-   *Where does he **works**?* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-5.png)*\n\n## Question words – Grammar chart\n\n![Educational chart for A1 elementary English learners displaying different question words such as 'who', 'where', 'when', 'why', 'what', 'how', including example dialogues for each.](/uploads/grammar/a1/questions/img-6.png)\n\n#### **What** + noun\n\nWe often use the question word **what + noun** as a question word: **what time, what colour, what size, etc.**\n\n-   ***What colour** is your jacket?*\n-   ***What size** do you want?*\n-   ***What car** does he have?*\n\n#### **How** + adjective/adverb\n\nWe can also use **how** **+ adjective/adverb** as a question word in phrases like **how often, how old, how tall****, etc.**\n\n-   ***How fast** is your car?*\n-   ***How big** is her house?*\n-   ***How often** do you play sports?*",
